  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in South Carolina total $7.5B in FY2026 to date. Agencies issued 14,241 awards, with an average award size of $528K, and obligations are down 9.7% year over year. Energy-related work dominates the state. The Department of Energy accounts for $4.5B of obligations, and Facilities Support Services is the largest industry at $3.5B. Major cleanup and construction sectors also show up: Remediation Services at $827M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $550M. Contractors tied to Savannah River operations lead the vendor list, with SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC at $3.3B and SAVANNAH RIVER MISSION COMPLETION, LLC at $812M. Defense and homeland agencies are active buyers as well, with the Department of Defense at $1.6B and the Department of Homeland Security at $494M. Other notable agency totals include Health and Human Services at $430M and Veterans Affairs at $291M. This snapshot covers partial fiscal-year data and uses contract obligations recorded so far in FY2026.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies are buying the most federal contracts in South Carolina?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy is the largest buyer at $4.5B. The Department of Defense follows at $1.6B, then the Department of Homeland Security at $494M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $430M. Other buyers with meaningful totals include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $291M and the Department of Agriculture at $70.2M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries capture the biggest shares of federal spending in South Carolina?",
        "body": "SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC is the top vendor at $3.3B, followed by SAVANNAH RIVER MISSION COMPLETION, LLC at $812M and BRASFIELD & GORRIE LLC at $326M. Facilities Support Services leads industries at $3.5B, with Remediation Services at $827M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $550M. Other industry totals include Engineering Services at $385M and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $357M."
      }
    ]
  },
